   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT authorizing the commissioner of general services to transfer and convey\ncertain unappropriated state land to the Sing Sing Prison Museum, county of\nWestchester",
    "basePrintNo": "S9425",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9425-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Westchester County",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9425\n\nSPONSOR: REICHLIN-MELNICK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act authorizing the commissioner of general services to transfer and\nconvey certain unappropriated state land to the Sing Sing Prison Museum,\ncounty of Westchester\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to allow the Sing Sing Prison Museum\n(SSPM) to lease certain lands within the Sing Sing Correctional Facili-\nty, specifically including that which contains the former Powerhouse,\nfor the purposes of establishing a museum.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Subject to the provisions of this act but notwithstanding any\nother provision of law to the contrary, the Commissioner of General\nServices is hereby authorized to lease a 99 year lease and convey to the\nSSPM the state land described in section two of this act, and shall\ngrant the museum an easement for access from the Powerhouse to the 1825\nCellblock. The Department of Corrections and Community Supervision shall\nenter into a management agreement with the SSPM to address the museums\noperating requirements, safety and security issues, and detailed proper-\nty boundaries for the leased property and easement.\n\nSection 2. Details the land authorized in section one.\n\nSection 3. States that the land described in Section 2 is not a legal\ndescription but is intended to identify the parcel conveyed. Section 4.\nThis shall not impair or result in diminution of the obligations to\nholders of any bonds which were financed, refinanced, or secured by\ncorrectional facilities, and shall not adversely affect any exemption of\ninterest on such bonds from Federal Income Tax.\n\nSection 5. An application must be made to the Office of General Services\nby the Department of Corrections and Community Supervision within one\nyear of the effective date of this act. The SSMP must adhere to state\nregulations pertaining to prevailing wages, the Americans with Disabili-\nty Act, and those relating to Minority and Women-owned Businesses.\n\nSection 6. Clarifies that the land in question will be used for the\npurposes of a museum. If the land is not used for an authorized purpose\ntitle of it shall revert to the state. Section 7. Establishes the effec-\ntive date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nYears ago, the concept of the Sing Sing Prison Museum (SSPM) was\ndiscussed by many in the community, not only to share with the public\nits deep history but to also show how the criminal justice and incarcer-\nation systems have changed throughout the years. These discussions have\nled to action, and a museum is in the works. As part of this project,\nthe museum will be acquiring (by lease) the Sing Sing Correctional\nFacility's Powerhouse, and access to the its original 1825 Cellblock.\nThis will be done in a way that ensures safety and security for all by\nhaving a management agreement in place between the SSPM and the Depart-\nment of Corrections and Community Supervision. This legislation specif-\nically allows the Commissioner of General Services to lease State land\nto the SSPM.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.7767-A of 2019/2020\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere are no direct costs to the state or local government in associ-\nation with the passage of this legislation.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
